item.description:
Immigrant rights and labor icon Dolores Huerta died Wednesday at the age of 96. Huerta co-founded the National Farm Workers Association in 1962 and then the United Farm Workers of America, working with Cesar Chavez. From 1965 to 1970, the union organized the historic Delano grape strike, which ended in major gains for farmworkers&#8217; labor rights.</p>
<p>In the years that followed, Huerta became heavily involved in the women&#8217;s liberation and immigrant rights movements and became a major supporter of the Democratic Party. In March, <em>The New York Times</em> published an investigation into Cesar Chavez&#8217;s history of sexual abuse of women and girls in the farmworkers&#8217; movement, prompting Huerta to reveal that she, too, had been raped by Chavez. Huerta said the assaults led to the births of two of her children.</p>
<p>Dolores Huerta appeared on <em>Democracy Now!</em> many times over the years. We remember her legacy with Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist Maria Hinojosa, who says, &#8220;Dolores was not only a labor organizer, a civil rights organizer, a feminist, committed, always optimistic about the possibilities of this country, but she loved life, and I want people to remember that.&#8221;

A competitive Senate race has gripped the once reliably Republican state of Alaska. If Democratic challenger Mary Peltola wins the election against incumbent Republican Senator Dan Sullivan, it could tip the balance of power in Washington. Attorneys for Peltola and the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee have submitted a motion to dismiss a Department of Justice lawsuit filed last week that seeks to block the certification of the election results in Alaska if Sullivan loses. President Trump confirmed on Friday he had ordered the <span class="caps">DOJ</span> to file the lawsuit, which alleges that the appearance of another candidate on the ballot with a similar name, Daniel J. Sullivan Jr., would mislead voters. The Alaska Supreme Court ruled ahead of the primary that Sullivan Jr., a retired schoolteacher who is running unaffiliated, could appear on the ballot.</p>
<p>The <span class="caps">ACLU</span> — on behalf of two Alaska voters, the League of Women Voters of Alaska and Common Cause — has moved to intervene in the case and has also filed a motion asking the court to dismiss it.</p>
<p>&#8220;There is no merit to this claim,&#8221; says Sophia Lin Lakin, director of the ACLU&#8217;s Voting Rights Project. &#8220;We&#8217;ve asked the court to consider sanctions, because the courts shouldn&#8217;t be used to launder baseless claims into distrust in our elections. There should be consequences for that.&#8221;

One of the most closely watched races in the midterm elections is in Michigan between Democrat Abdul El-Sayed and Republican Mike Rogers. El-Sayed is a former public health official who is campaigning on a platform of Medicare for All, ending military aid to Israel, and getting corporate money out of politics. Rogers is a former Republican congressman backed by President Trump.</p>
<p>If elected, El-Sayed would become the first Muslim to serve in the U.S. Senate. During Wednesday&#8217;s debate in Michigan, Rogers repeated the claim, without providing evidence, that El-Sayed has ties to the Muslim Brotherhood. El-Sayed has repeatedly denied the accusation.</p>
<p>&#8220;If Abdul El-Sayed wins, it will be a reminder that the left can win in a swing state like Michigan,&#8221; says Mehdi Hasan, editor-in-chief of <em>Zeteo</em>. &#8220;And it will be actually a moment to say, &#39;You know what? Americans rejected Islamophobia.&#39;&#8221;

We&#8217;re joined by Naomi Klein and Astra Taylor, co-authors of the book <em>End Times Fascism</em>, for a wide-ranging conversation on the multitrillion-dollar global investment in artificial intelligence and its reverberating effects around the world.</p>
<p>Klein recently spoke at the People&#8217;s AI Assembly, alongside other critics of the artificial intelligence industry, including New York City Mayor Zohran Mamdani and former chair of the Federal Trade Commission Lina Khan. &#8220;The resistance to AI is taking many forms, and it&#8217;s really a resistance to oligarch power, which is concentrated in the hands of these tech companies,&#8221; Klein tells <em>Democracy Now!</em> &#8220;After 50 years of outsourcing and hollowing out of public functions of the state, the very last stage is the outsourcing of thinking, of intelligence itself.&#8221;</p>
<p>Taylor, a co-founder of the Debt Collective, warns that the &#8220;circular&#8221; debt financing of much of the AI economy is &#8220;a waste of resources that also endangers the public.&#8221; The AI buildout &#8220;is so intense, so frenetic and so redundant&#8221; that it risks causing a major economic crash akin to the 2008 subprime mortgage crisis, Taylor says. If that happens, she adds, &#8220;Naomi and I think we need to use this moment, just like during the Great Depression and the New Deal era, to demand a real renovation, transformation, of our public services.&#8221;
